  ('5320440a-b1d1-466f-9634-2f7cc780514c','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','During which time period did the Neanderthal man live?','["Between 3-4 million years ago", "Between 1,00,000-40,000 years back", "Between 10,000-5,000 years back", "Between 500,000-200,000 years back"]'::jsonb,'The Neanderthal man lived between 1,00,000-40,000 years back.','BIO12EN06Q0155','active'),
  ('9679aad7-b8d2-4de2-ba88-4b6a0bff7deb','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did Neanderthal man use to protect their body?','["Tree leaves", "Animal hides", "Mud and clay", "Woven fabrics"]'::jsonb,'Neanderthal man used hides to protect their body.','BIO12EN06Q0156','active'),
  ('2e30ada4-48a8-4959-89cf-a90f21de700c','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','What practice did Neanderthal man follow for their dead?','["Cremation", "Exposing to vultures", "Burying their dead", "Preserving in caves"]'::jsonb,'Neanderthal man buried their dead.','BIO12EN06Q0157','active'),
  ('6db3972d-45e4-4435-b246-ae04ebe3af4e','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','Where did Homo sapiens first arise?','["Asia", "Europe", "Africa", "Australia"]'::jsonb,'Homo sapiens arose in Africa according to the text.','BIO12EN06Q0158','active'),
  ('37d503f6-4760-4dca-9d80-25709f11bdd9','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did Homo sapiens do after arising in Africa?','["Stayed only in Africa", "Moved across continents and developed into distinct races", "Went extinct immediately", "Evolved into Neanderthals"]'::jsonb,'Homo sapiens moved across continents and developed into distinct races.','BIO12EN06Q0159','active'),
  ('ef4b7490-ecb9-46ef-a5b6-4ab3951e8b6f','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','During which period did modern Homo sapiens arise?','["Between 1,00,000-40,000 years ago", "Between 75,000-10,000 years ago during ice age", "Between 3-4 million years ago", "Between 500,000-200,000 years ago"]'::jsonb,'Modern Homo sapiens arose during ice age between 75,000-10,000 years ago.','BIO12EN06Q0160','active'),
  ('691189cb-bb57-4d5c-970d-a019c1c3976a','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','When did pre-historic cave art develop?','["About 50,000 years ago", "About 18,000 years ago", "About 5,000 years ago", "About 100,000 years ago"]'::jsonb,'Pre-historic cave art developed about 18,000 years ago.','BIO12EN06Q0161','active'),
  ('440554e3-da2c-497d-920b-ca0262068f77','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Where can one see cave paintings by pre-historic humans in India?','["Ajanta caves in Maharashtra", "Bhimbetka rock shelter in Raisen district of Madhya Pradesh", "Elephanta caves in Mumbai", "Ellora caves in Aurangabad"]'::jsonb,'Cave paintings by pre-historic humans can be seen at Bhimbetka rock shelter in Raisen district of Madhya Pradesh.','BIO12EN06Q0162','active'),
  ('8544e25c-e376-4211-8ed9-47ca5d154445','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','When did agriculture come into practice?','["About 50,000 years back", "About 18,000 years back", "Around 10,000 years back", "About 1,00,000 years back"]'::jsonb,'Agriculture came around 10,000 years back according to the text.','BIO12EN06Q0163','active'),
  ('1ad3831f-b496-4d14-8db0-19753aedd7c3','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',0,'A','What started after agriculture came into practice?','["Human settlements", "Industrial revolution", "Space exploration", "Digital technology"]'::jsonb,'Human settlements started after agriculture came around 10,000 years back.','BIO12EN06Q0164','active'),
  ('ab2f9379-924b-4145-b570-20ea51005a88','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is the brain capacity range of Homo habilis?','["400-500cc", "650-800cc", "1000-1200cc", "1400cc"]'::jsonb,'The brain capacities of Homo habilis were between 650-800cc.','BIO12EN06Q0165','active'),
  ('f1af9ee9-f091-4b78-8b3f-f76f7461da2a','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What type of diet did Homo habilis probably follow?','["Carnivorous diet eating meat", "Herbivorous diet not eating meat", "Omnivorous diet eating everything", "Insectivorous diet eating insects"]'::jsonb,'Homo habilis probably did not eat meat.','BIO12EN06Q0166','active'),
  ('037cf851-9e3e-4c72-b20c-3a2dfb1c8025','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','When were fossils of Homo erectus discovered in Java?','["In 1859", "In 1891", "In 1900", "In 1953"]'::jsonb,'Fossils of Homo erectus were discovered in Java in 1891.','BIO12EN06Q0167','active'),
  ('b8777828-3c03-4df9-971d-254f2457ae12','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','How long ago did Homo erectus exist?','["About 3-4 million years ago", "About 1.5 million years ago", "About 500,000 years ago", "About 10,000 years ago"]'::jsonb,'Homo erectus existed about 1.5 million years ago.','BIO12EN06Q0168','active'),
  ('b247d0ea-ec12-44dd-a261-cb7562351d6e','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What characteristic of Homo erectus is mentioned regarding brain size?','["Small brain around 650cc", "Large brain around 900cc", "Very small brain around 400cc", "Brain same size as modern humans"]'::jsonb,'Homo erectus had a large brain around 900cc.','BIO12EN06Q0169','active'),
  ('889c1dad-15e6-444d-80a3-0e2838d67dbd','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did Neanderthal man use for body protection?','["Metal armor", "Animal hides", "Plant fibers", "Stone plates"]'::jsonb,'Neanderthal man used hides to protect their body.','BIO12EN06Q0170','active'),
  ('dae25bfc-114d-4a23-89dc-710508eaa3a7','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which hominid is described as the first human-like being?','["Homo erectus", "Homo habilis", "Neanderthal man", "Australopithecines"]'::jsonb,'Homo habilis was called the first human-like being the hominid.','BIO12EN06Q0171','active'),
  ('c3a57864-5e32-449c-bbb9-eb8e4a738ce2','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Where did Australopithecines probably live?','["In Asian forests", "In East African grasslands", "In European mountains", "In Australian deserts"]'::jsonb,'Australopithecines probably lived in East African grasslands.','BIO12EN06Q0172','active'),
  ('04a39c76-370a-46d2-bdf3-7062d2d48eed','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Against what background can the origin of life on earth be understood?','["The background of modern technology", "The background of origin of universe especially earth", "The background of human civilization", "The background of geological formations only"]'::jsonb,'The origin of life on earth can be understood only against the background of origin of universe especially earth.','BIO12EN06Q0173','active'),
  ('219c4cc7-c9ea-4499-82b8-fccc60f88a02','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What do most scientists believe preceded the appearance of the first cellular forms of life?','["Photosynthesis", "Chemical evolution i.e. formation of biomolecules", "Natural selection", "Genetic drift"]'::jsonb,'Most scientists believe chemical evolution, i.e., formation of biomolecules preceded the appearance of the first cellular forms of life.','BIO12EN06Q0174','active'),
  ('7faabf87-5a18-4c67-a047-ce9014c80712','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is the basis of the conjectured story about what happened to the first form of life?','["Lamarckian theory of use and disuse", "Darwinian ideas of organic evolution by natural selection", "Hugo de Vries theory of mutations", "Hardy-Weinberg principle"]'::jsonb,'The subsequent events as to what happened to the first form of life is a conjectured story based on Darwinian ideas of organic evolution by natural selection.','BIO12EN06Q0175','active'),
  ('5978c2cb-66e5-4d06-9a90-d6690f790bd9','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','How long has the diversity of life forms on earth been changing?','["Thousands of years", "Millions of years", "Hundreds of years", "Billions of years"]'::jsonb,'Diversity of life forms on earth has been changing over millions of years.','BIO12EN06Q0176','active'),
  ('3fe7157e-2f86-4a59-8acb-02a45a10db83','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is generally believed to result in variable fitness in a population?','["Genetic recombination only", "Variations in a population", "Gene migration only", "Natural disasters"]'::jsonb,'It is generally believed that variations in a population result in variable fitness.','BIO12EN06Q0177','active'),
  ('1377b3c1-8c6f-468c-a2b8-aed3d3de5984','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which phenomena may accentuate variations leading to appearance of new species?','["Photosynthesis and respiration", "Habitat fragmentation and genetic drift", "Cell division and mitosis", "Osmosis and diffusion"]'::jsonb,'Other phenomena like habitat fragmentation and genetic drift may accentuate these variations leading to appearance of new species and hence evolution.','BIO12EN06Q0178','active'),
  ('e19720ba-7400-4597-b030-e10533b0073c','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What accounts for homology according to the text?','["Convergent evolution", "The idea of branching descent", "Genetic mutations", "Natural selection alone"]'::jsonb,'Homology is accounted for by the idea of branching descent.','BIO12EN06Q0179','active'),
  ('c83db254-f98a-4aee-b481-d0e7708b359d','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which fields of study provide evidence for evolution?','["Physics and chemistry only", "Comparative anatomy, fossils and comparative biochemistry", "Mathematics and statistics only", "Geography and history only"]'::jsonb,'Study of comparative anatomy, fossils and comparative biochemistry provides evidence for evolution.','BIO12EN06Q0180','active'),
  ('272c0e6b-dfd5-435c-bf7b-fbbdbe461911','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which story of evolution is described as most interesting?','["Evolution of dinosaurs", "Evolution of modern man", "Evolution of birds", "Evolution of reptiles"]'::jsonb,'Among the stories of evolution of individual species, the story of evolution of modern man is most interesting.','BIO12EN06Q0181','active'),
  ('b791865c-110e-4117-b493-b0dfd165835c','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',0,'A','What does the evolution of modern man appear to parallel?','["Evolution of human brain and language", "Evolution of dinosaurs", "Evolution of plants", "Evolution of bacteria"]'::jsonb,'The story of evolution of modern man appears to parallel evolution of human brain and language.','BIO12EN06Q0182','active'),
  ('af0a1a7d-7c1a-4725-bef3-b53619c51ccd','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What phenomenon should be used to explain antibiotic resistance in bacteria?','["Lamarckian inheritance", "Darwinian selection theory", "Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium", "Panspermia theory"]'::jsonb,'Antibiotic resistance observed in bacteria should be explained in light of Darwinian selection theory.','BIO12EN06Q0183','active'),
  ('562390a5-6f1e-48bd-8ab0-7033194682a6','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',0,'A','What should students find out from newspapers and popular science articles?','["New fossil discoveries or controversies about evolution", "Sports news", "Political events", "Weather forecasts"]'::jsonb,'Students should find out from newspapers and popular science articles any new fossil discoveries or controversies about evolution.','BIO12EN06Q0184','active'),
  ('dddd0619-c3c3-4e41-baf4-49f6fad28dd8','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What clear definition should students attempt to give?','["Definition of cell", "Definition of the term species", "Definition of DNA", "Definition of protein"]'::jsonb,'Students should attempt giving a clear definition of the term species.','BIO12EN06Q0185','active'),
  ('219c1624-19b3-4860-8460-98c250786b52','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What components should be traced in human evolution?','["Only brain size", "Brain size and function, skeletal structure, dietary preference, etc.", "Only skeletal structure", "Only dietary preference"]'::jsonb,'Students should trace the various components of human evolution including brain size and function, skeletal structure, dietary preference, etc.','BIO12EN06Q0186','active'),
  ('43943ff9-8cd4-443f-a328-a3f6d12adb85','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',0,'A','What should students find out about animals other than man?','["Whether they have self-consciousness", "Whether they can fly", "Whether they can swim", "Whether they can hibernate"]'::jsonb,'Students should find out whether animals other than man have self-consciousness.','BIO12EN06Q0187','active'),
  ('7a35abaa-37e9-4036-8615-0e5351bf1913','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','How many modern-day animals should students list and link to ancient fossils?','["5 animals", "10 animals", "15 animals", "20 animals"]'::jsonb,'Students should list 10 modern-day animals and link them to corresponding ancient fossils.','BIO12EN06Q0188','active'),
  ('8fba46b5-d407-43ba-950c-7e30ebd55ee6','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What skill should students practise?','["Writing essays", "Drawing various animals and plants", "Solving mathematical problems", "Playing musical instruments"]'::jsonb,'Students should practise drawing various animals and plants.','BIO12EN06Q0189','active'),
  ('e7ac2629-ae4b-4194-8140-01ea49416f66','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',0,'A','What example should students describe?','["One example of adaptive radiation", "One example of convergent evolution", "One example of genetic drift", "One example of gene flow"]'::jsonb,'Students should describe one example of adaptive radiation.','BIO12EN06Q0190','active'),
  ('d3587711-f9d6-4cf6-9e72-bdcfcbc61721','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','Can human evolution be called adaptive radiation?','["Yes, without any doubt", "No, never", "This is a question for students to explore", "Only partially"]'::jsonb,'The text poses this as a question for students to explore: Can we call human evolution as adaptive radiation?','BIO12EN06Q0191','active'),
  ('5074ae81-b9e0-4aa8-80d6-99113f1babb4','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',0,'A','What should students trace using various resources?','["Evolutionary stages of any one animal", "Evolutionary stages of all animals", "Evolutionary stages of plants only", "Evolutionary stages of bacteria only"]'::jsonb,'Students should trace the evolutionary stages of any one animal using various resources.','BIO12EN06Q0192','active'),
  ('eeb211f6-9620-425b-b2d5-d2badc0e793a','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','According to the Big Bang theory, what happened immediately after the universe began to expand?','["Temperature increased rapidly", "Temperature came down", "Galaxies formed instantly", "Earth was created immediately"]'::jsonb,'The Big Bang theory states that the universe expanded and hence the temperature came down.','BIO12EN06Q0193','active'),
  ('2e7d5df1-4f24-4328-ad1c-62f953f72a33','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','Approximately how old is the universe according to the text?','["4.5 billion years", "500 million years", "13.8 billion years", "3 billion years"]'::jsonb,'The text states that the universe is almost 13.8 billion years old.','BIO12EN06Q0194','active'),
  ('eaf6369e-71ee-41f9-9546-b9e1d0f4266f','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What gases were present in the early atmosphere of Earth according to the text?','["Oxygen, Nitrogen, Carbon dioxide", "Water vapour, methane, carbon dioxide and ammonia", "Hydrogen, Helium, Oxygen", "Nitrogen, Oxygen, Argon"]'::jsonb,'The early atmosphere had water vapour, methane, carbon dioxide and ammonia released from molten mass.','BIO12EN06Q0195','active'),
  ('981f0f7c-ca6c-486f-827d-e3fd520dcca6','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is the Panspermia theory regarding the origin of life?','["Life originated from chemical reactions in the ocean", "Life came from outer space through spores", "Life arose from decaying organic matter", "Life was created by divine intervention"]'::jsonb,'Panspermia theory suggests that units of life called spores were transferred to different planets including Earth from outer space.','BIO12EN06Q0196','active'),
  ('53cf0dbe-ffd9-4a18-9d7f-ab7ab3cb9a8b','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did Louis Pasteur demonstrate through his experiments?','["Life can arise from non-living matter", "Life comes only from pre-existing life", "Spontaneous generation is possible in sterile conditions", "Bacteria can survive in vacuum"]'::jsonb,'Pasteur demonstrated that in pre-sterilised flasks, life did not come from killed yeast, proving life comes only from pre-existing life.','BIO12EN06Q0197','active'),
  ('1e65a4e4-81db-4b41-83ce-f4d1aa47d728','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Who proposed that the first form of life could have come from pre-existing non-living organic molecules?','["Charles Darwin and Alfred Wallace", "Oparin of Russia and Haldane of England", "S.L. Miller and Louis Pasteur", "Thomas Malthus and Hugo de Vries"]'::jsonb,'Oparin of Russia and Haldane of England proposed that the first form of life could have come from pre-existing non-living organic molecules.','BIO12EN06Q0198','active'),
  ('e11d3ff7-da2b-46f2-b115-4d3af1623808','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','What was the temperature maintained in S.L. Miller''s experiment?','["25 degrees Celsius", "100 degrees Celsius", "800 degrees Celsius", "1000 degrees Celsius"]'::jsonb,'Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing CH4, H2, NH3 and water vapour at 800 degrees Celsius.','BIO12EN06Q0199','active'),
  ('7b3985d2-cc5e-49bf-add5-e32906b3ef65','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What compounds were observed to form in Miller''s experiment?','["Only amino acids", "Amino acids, sugars, nitrogen bases, pigments and fats", "Only proteins and DNA", "Oxygen and ozone"]'::jsonb,'Miller observed formation of amino acids, and similar experiments showed formation of sugars, nitrogen bases, pigments and fats.','BIO12EN06Q0200','active'),
  ('32fd915f-e66e-4623-925b-e427513f39d1','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is meant by chemical evolution in the context of origin of life?','["Evolution of chemical elements over time", "Formation of diverse organic molecules from inorganic constituents", "Evolution of chemical reactions in living organisms", "Formation of minerals from organic compounds"]'::jsonb,'Chemical evolution refers to the formation of diverse organic molecules from inorganic constituents under conditions like high temperature and reducing atmosphere.','BIO12EN06Q0201','active'),
  ('4cc7cbe9-8528-4f06-ba1c-aa6c411b1dd4','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','When did the first cellular form of life possibly originate?','["500 million years ago", "About 2000 million years ago", "3 billion years ago", "4.5 billion years ago"]'::jsonb,'The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about 2000 million years ago.','BIO12EN06Q0202','active'),
  ('7a812f71-79b3-4f65-bf91-4e637babd54a','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Who proposed that the first form of life could have come from pre-existing non-living organic molecules?','["Charles Darwin and Alfred Wallace", "Oparin of Russia and Haldane of England", "Louis Pasteur and S.L. Miller", "Thomas Malthus and Ernst Haeckel"]'::jsonb,'Oparin of Russia and Haldane of England proposed that the first form of life could have come from pre-existing non-living organic molecules like RNA and protein, and that formation of life was preceded by chemical evolution.','BIO12EN06Q0203','active'),
  ('f030cf2e-ab74-401e-9c72-21b35ab52e9c','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What does the term ''chemical evolution'' refer to in the context of origin of life?','["Evolution of chemical elements in the periodic table", "Formation of diverse organic molecules from inorganic constituents", "Evolution of chemical bonds in complex molecules", "Formation of inorganic molecules from organic compounds"]'::jsonb,'Chemical evolution refers to the formation of diverse organic molecules from inorganic constituents under conditions of high temperature, volcanic storms, and reducing atmosphere containing CH4 and NH3.','BIO12EN06Q0204','active'),
  ('d551c17d-8ecd-408b-884c-255fbbdbdf4b','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','In S.L. Miller''s famous experiment, which gases were used in the closed flask?','["Oxygen, Nitrogen, Carbon dioxide and Water vapour", "CH4, H2, NH3 and Water vapour", "CO2, O2, N2 and Methane", "Helium, Neon, Argon and Water vapour"]'::jsonb,'S.L. Miller created electric discharge in a closed flask containing CH4, H2, NH3 and water vapour at 800°C to simulate early Earth conditions.','BIO12EN06Q0205','active'),
  ('4a8901a0-56fe-4eb6-bcac-a2587f2a0028','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What was the significant observation made by S.L. Miller in his laboratory experiment?','["Formation of DNA molecules", "Formation of amino acids", "Formation of complete cells", "Formation of fossil fuels"]'::jsonb,'S.L. Miller observed the formation of amino acids in his experiment. In similar experiments, others observed formation of sugars, nitrogen bases, pigments and fats.','BIO12EN06Q0206','active'),
  ('9698bac1-f104-4e8f-9bd5-727aec645ffc','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did the analysis of meteorite content reveal about the origin of organic compounds?','["Organic compounds are found only on Earth", "Similar compounds are occurring elsewhere in space", "Meteorites contain only inorganic materials", "Organic compounds cannot form in space"]'::jsonb,'Analysis of meteorite content revealed similar compounds indicating that similar processes are occurring elsewhere in space, supporting the idea that chemical evolution is not unique to Earth.','BIO12EN06Q0207','active'),
  ('6eb88c03-8f67-4fca-8fab-17175094086d','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','According to the text, when did the first non-cellular forms of life possibly originate?','["500 million years ago", "4.5 billion years ago", "3 billion years ago", "2000 million years ago"]'::jsonb,'The first non-cellular forms of life could have originated 3 billion years back. They would have been giant molecules like RNA, Protein, and Polysaccharides.','BIO12EN06Q0208','active'),
  ('9d67edb1-ed66-4e32-bc5a-576086f77b82','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','When did the first cellular form of life possibly originate according to the text?','["3 billion years ago", "500 million years ago", "2000 million years ago", "4.5 billion years ago"]'::jsonb,'The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about 2000 million years ago. These were probably single-cells and all life forms were in water environment only.','BIO12EN06Q0209','active'),
  ('4a7ed340-c920-4d0b-b291-62f810422aa9','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','What theory did Louis Pasteur''s experiment disprove?','["Theory of natural selection", "Theory of chemical evolution", "Theory of spontaneous generation", "Theory of adaptive radiation"]'::jsonb,'Louis Pasteur demonstrated that life comes only from pre-existing life, disproving the theory of spontaneous generation which claimed life came out of decaying and rotting matter.','BIO12EN06Q0210','active'),
  ('c22f489c-73cd-4fc7-b174-a643bc976c88','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What are fossils according to the text?','["Soft tissues of organisms preserved in ice", "Remains of hard parts of life-forms found in rocks", "Impressions of organisms in mud", "DNA samples extracted from ancient organisms"]'::jsonb,'Fossils are defined as remains of hard parts of life-forms found in rocks.','BIO12EN06Q0211','active'),
  ('f575d47d-9d06-45a0-83f3-8b73c500e8db','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What does a cross-section of earth''s crust indicate?','["The temperature of the earth''s core", "The arrangement of sediments one over the other during the long history of earth", "The distribution of minerals in the earth", "The depth of oceans on earth"]'::jsonb,'A cross-section of earth''s crust indicates the arrangement of sediments one over the other during the long history of earth.','BIO12EN06Q0212','active'),
  ('0b1cee17-7af0-4e66-93da-8c6a6ba8109e','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What do different-aged rock sediments contain?','["Different types of minerals", "Fossils of different life-forms", "Various types of crystals", "Different soil compositions"]'::jsonb,'Different-aged rock sediments contain fossils of different life-forms who probably died during the formation of the particular sediment.','BIO12EN06Q0213','active'),
  ('e137fd45-4f7f-4992-8095-0a54a3d549b3','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which organisms are given as examples of extinct organisms?','["Whales and bats", "Dinosaurs", "Cheetahs and humans", "Fish and amphibians"]'::jsonb,'Dinosaurs are given as examples of extinct organisms in the text.','BIO12EN06Q0214','active'),
  ('91ae4d8b-4cbb-46e4-a492-e4268153bec8','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is paleontological evidence?','["Study of embryos of different organisms", "Study of fossils in different sedimentary layers indicating geological periods", "Comparison of anatomy of modern organisms", "Study of genetic mutations in populations"]'::jsonb,'Paleontological evidence refers to the study of fossils in different sedimentary layers that indicates the geological period in which they existed.','BIO12EN06Q0215','active'),
  ('60623784-9d14-4329-8778-3fbed781cf23','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','How are the ages of fossils calculated?','["By comparing with modern organisms", "By radioactive-dating method", "By studying the sediment composition", "By examining the fossil size"]'::jsonb,'The ages of fossils are calculated using the method of radioactive-dating.','BIO12EN06Q0216','active'),
  ('97f6af25-9045-43b1-80db-4966e0005869','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Who proposed embryological support for evolution?','["Charles Darwin", "Ernst Haeckel", "Karl Ernst von Baer", "Alfred Wallace"]'::jsonb,'Ernst Haeckel proposed embryological support for evolution based on observations of features during embryonic stage.','BIO12EN06Q0217','active'),
  ('7432e2fb-1230-4ad9-9d19-b7718778c951','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What common feature did Haeckel observe in embryos of all vertebrates?','["Development of wings", "Development of a row of vestigial gill slits behind the head", "Presence of tail in all embryos", "Development of four limbs"]'::jsonb,'Haeckel observed that embryos of all vertebrates develop a row of vestigial gill slits just behind the head.','BIO12EN06Q0218','active'),
  ('94e89730-de4a-4974-915a-f7e58b4e58db','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','In which organism is the gill slit a functional organ?','["Humans", "Whales", "Fish", "Bats"]'::jsonb,'The gill slit is a functional organ only in fish and not found in any other adult vertebrates.','BIO12EN06Q0219','active'),
  ('13c345fc-a622-4c22-b710-8bf31ef928ce','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Who disapproved Haeckel''s embryological proposal?','["Charles Darwin", "Karl Ernst von Baer", "Louis Pasteur", "Alfred Wallace"]'::jsonb,'Karl Ernst von Baer disapproved Haeckel''s proposal on careful study.','BIO12EN06Q0220','active'),
  ('4a454aaa-71ba-453d-bbee-212e3564abc4','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did Karl Ernst von Baer note about embryos?','["Embryos pass through adult stages of other organisms", "Embryos never pass through the adult stages of other organisms", "Embryos are identical to adult organisms", "Embryos develop faster than adult organisms"]'::jsonb,'Karl Ernst von Baer noted that embryos never pass through the adult stages of other organisms.','BIO12EN06Q0221','active'),
  ('fe2c6d65-c8d7-4a5a-bf85-af11993f0fe8','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',2,'C','What does comparative anatomy and morphology show?','["Only similarities among organisms", "Only differences among organisms", "Similarities and differences among organisms of today and those that existed years ago", "Only genetic variations among organisms"]'::jsonb,'Comparative anatomy and morphology shows similarities and differences among organisms of today and those that existed years ago.','BIO12EN06Q0222','active'),
  ('46a0e563-8edf-4783-a575-a990286a36cb','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which mammals are mentioned as sharing similarities in forelimb bone pattern?','["Elephants, tigers, lions and bears", "Whales, bats, cheetah and human", "Dogs, cats, horses and cows", "Monkeys, apes, gorillas and chimpanzees"]'::jsonb,'Whales, bats, cheetah and human are mentioned as sharing similarities in the pattern of bones of forelimbs.','BIO12EN06Q0223','active'),
  ('66e35f02-ea08-488a-af15-9062b46d44fe','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What bones are present in the forelimbs of whales, bats, cheetah and human?','["Femur, tibia, fibula, tarsals, metatarsals", "Humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals and phalanges", "Skull, ribs, vertebrae, sternum", "Pelvis, sacrum, coccyx, patella"]'::jsonb,'All of them have humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals and phalanges in their forelimbs.','BIO12EN06Q0224','active'),
  ('b802d9a8-9c7c-45a8-a6a4-56b50117baa9','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is divergent evolution?','["Different structures developing similar functions", "Same structure developing along different directions due to adaptations to different needs", "Evolution of organisms in the same direction", "Evolution of similar species from different ancestors"]'::jsonb,'Divergent evolution is when the same structure develops along different directions due to adaptations to different needs.','BIO12EN06Q0225','active'),
  ('fdc3eb11-b9b9-4a21-8444-8a7be3295009','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What are homologous structures?','["Structures with different origins but similar functions", "Structures with similar anatomical structure but different functions", "Structures that are identical in all organisms", "Structures that have no function"]'::jsonb,'Homologous structures have similar anatomical structure but perform different functions.','BIO12EN06Q0226','active'),
  ('8adcb797-478c-43cb-a1c6-b701f2d2a711','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What does homology indicate?','["Different origins of organisms", "Common ancestry", "Convergent evolution", "Random mutations"]'::jsonb,'Homology indicates common ancestry among organisms.','BIO12EN06Q0227','active'),
  ('b175cfd8-4f06-41c9-9354-cbdba69d4128','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What other examples of homologous structures are mentioned?','["Vertebrate legs and arms", "Vertebrate hearts or brains", "Insect wings and bird wings", "Fish scales and reptile scales"]'::jsonb,'Other examples mentioned are vertebrate hearts or brains.','BIO12EN06Q0228','active'),
  ('e6b9f945-b51e-4048-a9ad-739b76b012f7','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What type of evidence is the study of fossils in sedimentary layers?','["Embryological evidence", "Paleontological evidence", "Comparative anatomical evidence", "Biochemical evidence"]'::jsonb,'The study of fossils in different sedimentary layers is called paleontological evidence.','BIO12EN06Q0229','active'),
  ('2bfc79ca-6231-41ce-bd64-f3b0498ca3b0','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did the study of fossils show about life-forms?','["Life-forms have remained unchanged over time", "Life-forms varied over time and certain life forms are restricted to certain geological timespans", "All life-forms existed at the same time", "Modern organisms are identical to ancient organisms"]'::jsonb,'The study showed that life-forms varied over time and certain life forms are restricted to certain geological timespans.','BIO12EN06Q0230','active'),
  ('c318f4bf-765e-4d67-8ab9-ead080eef962','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What can similarities among organisms be interpreted to understand?','["The age of the organisms", "Whether common ancestors were shared or not", "The habitat of the organisms", "The diet of the organisms"]'::jsonb,'Similarities can be interpreted to understand whether common ancestors were shared or not.','BIO12EN06Q0231','active'),
  ('f3116a43-78d2-4c16-ace4-d6fcb356b9f8','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which mammals share similarities in the pattern of bones of forelimbs?','["Elephants, tigers, lions and bears", "Whales, bats, cheetah and human", "Dogs, cats, horses and cows", "Monkeys, apes, gorillas and chimpanzees"]'::jsonb,'Whales, bats, cheetah and human share similarities in the pattern of bones of forelimbs.','BIO12EN06Q0232','active'),
  ('600f7f4f-a5f6-46b2-833d-5598ed27b472','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Do the forelimbs of whales, bats, cheetah and human perform the same function?','["Yes, they all perform the same function", "No, they perform different functions", "Yes, they all are used for flying", "Yes, they all are used for swimming"]'::jsonb,'These forelimbs perform different functions in these animals though they have similar anatomical structure.','BIO12EN06Q0233','active'),
  ('5e85bdd6-cc17-46b7-80c1-f0991d775cea','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What bones are present in the forelimbs of whales, bats, cheetah and human?','["Femur, tibia, fibula, tarsals, metatarsals", "Humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals and phalanges", "Skull, ribs, vertebrae, sternum", "Pelvis, sacrum, coccyx, patella"]'::jsonb,'All of them have humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals and phalanges in their forelimbs.','BIO12EN06Q0234','active'),
  ('2447cba8-64ff-43f0-ae82-c9759b56cc19','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is divergent evolution?','["Different structures developing similar functions", "Same structure developing along different directions due to adaptations to different needs", "Evolution of organisms in the same direction", "Evolution of similar species from different ancestors"]'::jsonb,'Divergent evolution is when the same structure develops along different directions due to adaptations to different needs.','BIO12EN06Q0235','active'),
  ('6a505b36-0702-4fa1-9a5d-39061a167ee6','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What are homologous structures?','["Structures with different origins but similar functions", "Structures with similar anatomical structure but different functions", "Structures that are identical in all organisms", "Structures that have no function"]'::jsonb,'Homologous structures have similar anatomical structure but perform different functions.','BIO12EN06Q0236','active'),
  ('cc296cbd-47f6-4967-a1e7-c7cdc1d98bd2','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What does homology indicate?','["Different origins of organisms", "Common ancestry", "Convergent evolution", "Random mutations"]'::jsonb,'Homology indicates common ancestry among organisms.','BIO12EN06Q0237','active'),
  ('ae15b48d-f4a9-43d5-b4de-290426e8dad1','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What other examples of homologous structures are mentioned?','["Vertebrate legs and arms", "Vertebrate hearts or brains", "Insect wings and bird wings", "Fish scales and reptile scales"]'::jsonb,'Other examples mentioned are vertebrate hearts or brains.','BIO12EN06Q0238','active'),
  ('ef45c622-7232-4388-90d0-5a381d608b3d','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is convergent evolution?','["Same structure developing different functions", "Different structures evolving for the same function and hence having similarity", "Evolution of identical species from common ancestors", "Evolution caused by genetic mutations only"]'::jsonb,'Convergent evolution is when different structures evolve for the same function and hence have similarity.','BIO12EN06Q0239','active'),
  ('4e326de8-5a80-46cd-a772-f040727f3969','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which structures are given as examples of analogous structures?','["Forelimbs of whale and bat", "Wings of butterflies and birds", "Hearts of vertebrates", "Brains of mammals"]'::jsonb,'Wings of butterflies and birds are given as examples of analogous structures.','BIO12EN06Q0240','active'),
  ('b96b1499-78f8-41a5-8d00-587ddc9ce398','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Are the wings of butterflies and birds anatomically similar?','["Yes, they are anatomically similar", "No, they are not anatomically similar though they appear alike in function", "Yes, they have identical bone structures", "Yes, they evolved from the same ancestor"]'::jsonb,'They are not anatomically similar structures though they appear alike in their function of flight.','BIO12EN06Q0241','active'),
  ('570ea3d7-0679-455d-b5c4-039ae9952e31','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What are analogous structures a result of?','["Divergent evolution", "Convergent evolution", "Genetic drift", "Natural selection only"]'::jsonb,'Analogous structures are a result of convergent evolution.','BIO12EN06Q0242','active'),
  ('5b926dc5-6e54-45f7-97c1-27550b48c0d3','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is industrial melanism an example of?','["Genetic drift", "Natural selection", "Mutation", "Gene flow"]'::jsonb,'Industrial melanism is an example of natural selection.','BIO12EN06Q0243','active'),
  ('99410766-124b-4fae-ab99-156692e4a18e','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Before industrialisation, which type of moths were more common on trees?','["Dark-winged or melanised moths", "White-winged moths", "Red-winged moths", "Yellow-winged moths"]'::jsonb,'Before industrialisation, there were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged or melanised moths.','BIO12EN06Q0244','active'),
  ('19c6d3e3-d692-4e60-87d5-2e57eeca11f4','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Why did tree trunks become dark after industrialisation?','["Due to increased rainfall", "Due to industrial smoke and soots", "Due to volcanic eruptions", "Due to forest fires"]'::jsonb,'After industrialisation, the tree trunks became dark due to industrial smoke and soots.','BIO12EN06Q0245','active'),
  ('4914320e-6ffb-4cbc-b255-ff4a554941be','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Why did the white-winged moth not survive after industrialisation?','["Due to lack of food", "Due to predators spotting them against the contrasting dark background", "Due to climate change", "Due to disease"]'::jsonb,'The white-winged moth did not survive due to predators as they were spotted against the contrasting dark background.','BIO12EN06Q0246','active'),
  ('d5944261-b98e-47ff-af54-3f19a9d6c8e0','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Why did the dark-winged or melanised moth survive after industrialisation?','["They had better flying ability", "They could camouflage themselves against the dark tree trunks", "They ate more food", "They were resistant to pollution"]'::jsonb,'Dark-winged or melanised moths survived because they could camouflage themselves against the dark tree trunks.','BIO12EN06Q0247','active'),
  ('063e2b42-d35c-4914-b416-b6e4d564638f','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What covered the trees before industrialisation?','["Industrial soot", "Thick growth of almost white-coloured lichen", "Green moss", "Brown algae"]'::jsonb,'Before industrialisation, thick growth of almost white-coloured lichen covered the trees.','BIO12EN06Q0248','active'),
  ('29364cbd-cca1-4aff-88e8-c42cf596f880','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','In the white lichen background, which moths were picked out by predators?','["White-winged moths", "Dark-coloured moths", "Both types equally", "Neither type"]'::jsonb,'In the white lichen background, the dark-coloured moths were picked out by predators.','BIO12EN06Q0249','active'),
  ('31a59168-1b8f-4bcc-af97-6f56becc5de9','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What can lichens be used as?','["Food source for insects", "Industrial pollution indicators", "Medicinal herbs", "Soil fertilizers"]'::jsonb,'Lichens can be used as industrial pollution indicators.','BIO12EN06Q0250','active'),
  ('9cf5702c-8085-43f2-b77b-a494caa2da06','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Why will lichens not grow in polluted areas?','["They need pure water only", "They are sensitive to pollution and cannot grow in polluted areas", "They require high temperatures", "They need specific soil types"]'::jsonb,'Lichens will not grow in areas that are polluted, making them good pollution indicators.','BIO12EN06Q0251','active'),
  ('6def863c-555d-4396-85f4-85cca6535de8','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What does camouflage mean in the context of moths?','["Ability to fly faster", "Ability to hide in the background", "Ability to produce more offspring", "Ability to change colour instantly"]'::jsonb,'Moths that were able to camouflage themselves, i.e., hide in the background, survived.','BIO12EN06Q0252','active'),
  ('f6fd32d7-d66f-4f95-8f10-1ede0f09844c','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Where was the count of melanic moths low?','["In heavily industrialised cities", "In rural areas where industrialisation did not occur", "In tropical forests", "In desert regions"]'::jsonb,'In areas where industrialisation did not occur, such as rural areas, the count of melanic moths was low.','BIO12EN06Q0253','active'),
  ('d9b8fbe4-7de5-4a6d-9a1c-f5e520b5afac','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What does the moth example show about mixed populations?','["All variants survive equally", "Those that can better-adapt survive and increase in population size", "Larger organisms always survive", "Only the strongest predators survive"]'::jsonb,'In a mixed population, those that can better-adapt survive and increase in population size.','BIO12EN06Q0254','active'),
  ('a9e8e377-b65b-440e-95b2-85de9db50f89','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What has excess use of herbicides and pesticides resulted in?','["Extinction of all pest species", "Selection of resistant varieties", "Increase in crop yield only", "Complete elimination of weeds"]'::jsonb,'Excess use of herbicides and pesticides has resulted in selection of resistant varieties.','BIO12EN06Q0255','active'),
  ('3daea208-031e-4f64-a738-7c2de74fda2f','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','How quickly do resistant organisms appear compared to natural evolution?','["In centuries", "In months or years, not centuries", "In millions of years", "Instantaneously"]'::jsonb,'Resistant organisms are appearing in a time scale of months or years and not centuries.','BIO12EN06Q0256','active'),
  ('e5758656-fd38-499c-b5c8-16b600344527','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What are examples of evolution by anthropogenic action?','["Evolution caused by natural disasters", "Evolution caused by human activities like use of herbicides, pesticides and antibiotics", "Evolution caused by climate change only", "Evolution caused by volcanic eruptions"]'::jsonb,'Examples include evolution caused by excess use of herbicides, pesticides, antibiotics and drugs.','BIO12EN06Q0257','active'),
  ('9bc60fd1-ce9f-465a-bd71-1a5b0dabc799','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Is evolution a directed process according to the text?','["Yes, it is completely directed", "No, it is not a directed process in the sense of determinism", "Yes, it follows a predetermined path", "Yes, it is controlled by humans"]'::jsonb,'Evolution is not a directed process in the sense of determinism. It is a stochastic process.','BIO12EN06Q0258','active'),
  ('ba6daadf-a84a-4332-b077-6876c2dd8907','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What kind of process is evolution described as?','["A deterministic process", "A stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation", "A completely predictable process", "A human-controlled process"]'::jsonb,'Evolution is a stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation.','BIO12EN06Q0259','active'),
  ('33dc7c7b-f31e-4c0e-82cd-6226c420497e','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What was the explanation given for the observation of different colored moths?','["Moths changed color due to genetic mutation", "Predators will spot a moth against a contrasting background", "Climate change caused moth color variation", "Industrial chemicals directly changed moth color"]'::jsonb,'The explanation put forth was that predators will spot a moth against a contrasting background, making camouflage crucial for survival.','BIO12EN06Q0260','active'),
  ('736e8031-38bd-4287-813c-c770e85b4ebc','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Why did tree trunks become dark during the postindustrialisation period?','["Due to natural forest fires", "Due to industrial smoke and soots", "Due to volcanic eruptions", "Due to acid rain"]'::jsonb,'During the postindustrialisation period, tree trunks became dark due to industrial smoke and soots.','BIO12EN06Q0261','active'),
  ('f7cb42d6-804c-47a5-a206-680b17a7fbf9','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which moth survived when tree trunks became dark after industrialisation?','["White-winged moth", "Dark-winged or melanised moth", "Both types survived equally", "Neither type survived"]'::jsonb,'Under dark conditions, the white-winged moth did not survive due to predators, while the dark-winged or melanised moth survived.','BIO12EN06Q0262','active'),
  ('6b0f2cbb-6cd2-45e1-b0e9-82adde2a6143','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What covered the trees before industrialisation set in?','["Industrial soot", "Thick growth of almost white-coloured lichen", "Dark moss", "Red algae"]'::jsonb,'Before industrialisation, thick growth of almost white-coloured lichen covered the trees.','BIO12EN06Q0263','active'),
  ('c732464d-fd2c-4fee-8df3-306d77ef8583','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','In the white lichen background, which moth survived?','["Dark-coloured moth", "White-winged moth", "Both moths equally", "Neither moth survived"]'::jsonb,'In the white lichen background, the white-winged moth survived but the dark-coloured moths were picked out by predators.','BIO12EN06Q0264','active'),
  ('46ead018-ccd6-4356-9dc3-b69e77a318ff','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What can lichens be used as?','["Food source for animals", "Industrial pollution indicators", "Medicinal herbs", "Building materials"]'::jsonb,'Lichens can be used as industrial pollution indicators because they will not grow in polluted areas.','BIO12EN06Q0265','active'),
  ('6ad847f4-bf97-4b20-8503-a219a3d78273','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Why do lichens not grow in polluted areas?','["They require pure water only", "They are sensitive to pollution", "They need high temperatures", "They compete with other plants"]'::jsonb,'Lichens will not grow in areas that are polluted, making them sensitive indicators of industrial pollution.','BIO12EN06Q0266','active'),
  ('081e9fb2-c3ea-448d-807f-59620baa48a9','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What does the moth example demonstrate about mixed populations?','["All variants are completely wiped out", "Those that can better-adapt survive and increase in population size", "Only the largest individuals survive", "Mutation is the only factor for survival"]'::jsonb,'The moth example showed that in a mixed population, those that can better-adapt survive and increase in population size.','BIO12EN06Q0267','active'),
  ('e64b3c34-cb4f-4477-8846-66ed68eaa830','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Is any variant completely wiped out according to the text?','["Yes, all less-adapted variants are wiped out", "No variant is completely wiped out", "Only the dominant variant survives", "Variants are wiped out every century"]'::jsonb,'The text states that no variant is completely wiped out in a mixed population.','BIO12EN06Q0268','active'),
  ('0c54d8f4-e6aa-4834-946e-770a8bdff295','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What has excess use of herbicides and pesticides resulted in?','["Complete elimination of pests", "Selection of resistant varieties", "Increased crop yield", "Soil fertility improvement"]'::jsonb,'Excess use of herbicides and pesticides has resulted in selection of resistant varieties in a much lesser time scale.','BIO12EN06Q0269','active'),
  ('1f812aeb-9a01-4d1b-9c9e-5c6f0a17004a','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','How quickly do resistant organisms appear when using antibiotics?','["In centuries", "In months or years", "In decades only", "They never appear"]'::jsonb,'Resistant organisms appear in a time scale of months or years and not centuries.','BIO12EN06Q0270','active'),
  ('4d1edfd9-7ee9-4302-b840-b336e529146f','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What are examples of evolution by anthropogenic action?','["Natural selection in wild animals", "Resistant organisms appearing due to human use of chemicals and drugs", "Climate change effects on plants", "Volcanic eruption impacts"]'::jsonb,'Examples of evolution by anthropogenic action include resistant organisms appearing due to human use of herbicides, pesticides, antibiotics, and drugs.','BIO12EN06Q0271','active'),
  ('83021099-f77b-493e-9729-9117786e5c93','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Is evolution a directed process according to determinism?','["Yes, evolution follows a predetermined path", "No, evolution is not a directed process in the sense of determinism", "Evolution is directed only in microbes", "Evolution is directed in animals but not plants"]'::jsonb,'Evolution is not a directed process in the sense of determinism; it is a stochastic process based on chance events.','BIO12EN06Q0272','active'),
  ('fad446af-32c5-486c-bf69-2dded1e3533e','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What type of process is evolution described as?','["A deterministic process", "A stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation", "A linear process with fixed outcomes", "A controlled laboratory process"]'::jsonb,'Evolution is described as a stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation in organisms.','BIO12EN06Q0273','active'),
  ('7b4ce284-325b-4060-b865-69195d77c36c','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Where did Darwin observe an amazing diversity of creatures?','["Amazon rainforest", "Galapagos Islands", "Australian continent", "African savannah"]'::jsonb,'During his journey, Darwin went to Galapagos Islands where he observed an amazing diversity of creatures.','BIO12EN06Q0274','active'),
  ('8df1e9a0-da2c-4a06-86d3-b741900256dd','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What small black birds amazed Darwin in the Galapagos Islands?','["Sparrows", "Darwin''s Finches", "Crows", "Penguins"]'::jsonb,'Small black birds later called Darwin''s Finches amazed Darwin with their diversity in the Galapagos Islands.','BIO12EN06Q0275','active'),
  ('f2f92b16-2a9e-4463-b1d3-7fd3f9849763','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did Darwin conjecture about the finch varieties?','["They were brought by humans", "All varieties evolved on the island itself", "They migrated from other continents", "They were created separately"]'::jsonb,'Darwin conjectured that all the varieties of finches evolved on the island itself from an original seed-eating form.','BIO12EN06Q0276','active'),
  ('4860809b-b7b7-4f20-947e-a92e99550d7d','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','From what original feature did the finch varieties evolve?','["Insect-eating features", "Seed-eating features", "Meat-eating features", "Fruit-eating features"]'::jsonb,'From the original seed-eating features, many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become insectivorous and vegetarian finches.','BIO12EN06Q0277','active'),
  ('8595f12c-6bea-474b-b150-c10fbed978e4','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is adaptive radiation?','["Evolution of a single species in isolation", "Evolution of different species in a given geographical area starting from a point and radiating to other habitats", "Migration of species to new continents", "Extinction of species due to climate change"]'::jsonb,'Adaptive radiation is the process of evolution of different species in a given geographical area starting from a point and radiating to other areas of geography.','BIO12EN06Q0278','active'),
  ('47c387d6-f5d5-49cb-9849-a42507bf83d8','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Which birds represent one of the best examples of adaptive radiation?','["Sparrows", "Darwin''s finches", "Eagles", "Parrots"]'::jsonb,'Darwin''s finches represent one of the best examples of the phenomenon of adaptive radiation.','BIO12EN06Q0279','active'),
  ('fefd7ec3-c3d3-4a71-9c16-f6a8a9ae0d92','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What is another example of adaptive radiation mentioned in the text?','["African elephants", "Australian marsupials", "Arctic foxes", "Amazon frogs"]'::jsonb,'Another example of adaptive radiation mentioned is Australian marsupials that evolved from an ancestral stock within the Australian island continent.','BIO12EN06Q0280','active'),
  ('e1a61875-261e-48d4-bda5-fd39035ca3e5','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',0,'A','Where did Australian marsupials evolve from an ancestral stock?','["Within the Australian island continent", "From South America", "From Africa", "From Asia"]'::jsonb,'Australian marsupials evolved from an ancestral stock, but all within the Australian island continent.','BIO12EN06Q0281','active'),
  ('235c3dd0-7dcd-464d-b7b4-7b6ec3660526','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What enabled the finches to become insectivorous and vegetarian?','["Changes in their digestive system", "Altered beaks that arose from original seed-eating features", "Migration to different islands", "Changes in their wing structure"]'::jsonb,'From the original seed-eating features, many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become insectivorous and vegetarian finches.','BIO12EN06Q0282','active'),
  ('69373d5a-5e8d-407e-9d7c-f9a30ebaf050','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What happens when more than one adaptive radiation occurs in an isolated geographical area?','["Species extinction", "Convergent evolution", "Genetic drift", "Founder effect"]'::jsonb,'When more than one adaptive radiation appeared to have occurred in an isolated geographical area, it can be called convergent evolution.','BIO12EN06Q0283','active'),
  ('4143a871-0da0-491b-a3a9-76ba9d81469d','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Where did Darwin go during his journey where he observed amazing diversity of creatures?','["Hawaiian Islands", "Galapagos Islands", "Canary Islands", "Falkland Islands"]'::jsonb,'During his journey, Darwin went to Galapagos Islands where he observed an amazing diversity of creatures.','BIO12EN06Q0284','active'),
  ('ea43e894-594c-4bf6-b6a7-b6c748d8a485','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What small black birds amazed Darwin during his visit to Galapagos Islands?','["Galapagos penguins", "Darwin''s Finches", "Galapagos hawks", "Black mockingbirds"]'::jsonb,'Small black birds later called Darwin''s Finches amazed Darwin during his visit to Galapagos Islands.','BIO12EN06Q0285','active'),
  ('f25bbbc2-f90a-48ef-a033-142cf6b7fbf9','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What did Darwin realize about finches on the same island?','["All finches were identical", "There were many varieties of finches in the same island", "Finches were only seed-eating", "Finches were extinct"]'::jsonb,'Darwin realized that there were many varieties of finches in the same island.','BIO12EN06Q0286','active'),
  ('b2a0d432-b53e-494b-aee7-b25b25ad12d5','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','Where did Darwin conjecture that all varieties of finches evolved?','["On the mainland of South America", "On the island itself", "In North America", "In Africa"]'::jsonb,'Darwin conjectured that all varieties of finches evolved on the island itself.','BIO12EN06Q0287','active'),
  ('c2528c99-5e0f-4774-b20d-eaf0feb02f4a','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What was the original feature of the finches before evolution?','["Insect-eating features", "Seed-eating features", "Carnivorous features", "Fruit-eating features"]'::jsonb,'The original feature of the finches was seed-eating before they evolved into other forms.','BIO12EN06Q0288','active'),
  ('72d65ef0-0c49-4741-a94a-5efc2a8ffc01','English','12th','Biology','bio-ch06','Molecular Basis of Inheritance',1,'B','What changes in beaks enabled finches to become insectivorous and vegetarian?','["Smaller beaks", "Altered beaks", "Curved beaks", "Hooked beaks"]'::jsonb,'Many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become insectivorous and vegetarian finches.','BIO12EN06Q0289','active'),
